  {
    "question_id": "64b169f0f23ac7d519f9afdc",
    "type": "singleCorrect",
    "difficulty_level": 1,
    "syllabus_category": "noChange",
    "question_structure": "default",
    "paper_year_title": "JEE Advanced 2009 (Paper 1)",
    "paper_year_short": "2009 (Paper 1)",
    "held_on": "2009-04-30T09:00:00.000Z",
    "question_text": "The correct acidity order of the following is\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/7b6qdMHsSZY6-VDA-Ft2vqn1SuYfdt-U_Nh6myMTsgA.original.fullsize.png\"><br>",
    "question_image": null,
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "zv8Ql",
        "text": "\nIII $>$ IV $>$ II $>$ I\n",
        "image": null
      },
      {
        "id": "H1Azj",
        "text": "\nIV $>$ III $>$ I $>$ II\n",
        "image": null
      },
      {
        "id": "RzL67",
        "text": "\nIII $>$ II $>$ I $>$ IV\n",
        "image": null
      },
      {
        "id": "A2aLu",
        "text": "\nII $>$ III $>$ IV $>$ I",
        "image": null
      }
    ],
    "correct_answer": "zv8Ql",
    "solution_text": "A carboxylic acid is stronger acid than phenol, hence both III and IV are stronger acids than both I and II. Also IV has a methyl group that gives electron donating inductive effect and decreases the acid strength. Therefore, III is stronger acid that IV. Between I and II, the dominant electron withdrawing inductive effect of chlorine increases acid strength of phenol slightly, hence II is stronger acid than I. Thus, the overall order is :\n(a) III $>$ IV $>$ II $>$ I",
    "solution_image": null,
    "topic": "Preparation and Properties of Carboxylic Acids"
  },
  {
    "question_id": "6a0be51044d287c9361e5ca2",
    "type": "numerical",
    "difficulty_level": 2,
    "syllabus_category": "noChange",
    "question_structure": "default",
    "paper_year_title": "JEE Advanced 2026 (Paper 1)",
    "paper_year_short": "2026 (Paper 1)",
    "held_on": "2026-05-25T09:00:00.000Z",
    "question_text": "The sum of total number of carbonyl groups ($\\text{>C=O}$) present in the major products $\\textbf{X}$ and $\\textbf{Y}$ in the following reactions is ____.<br><img style=\"width: 500px\" src=\"https://cdn.quizrr.in/question-assets/jee_adv/2026/watermarked_images/p1_english__q43_d1.png\" />",
    "question_image": null,
    "options": [],
    "correct_answer": "4",
    "solution_text": "Reaction 1:<br>The reactant is 2,4-dimethyl-3-oxopentanedioic acid, which is a $\\beta$-keto acid (both $-\\text{CO}_2\\text{H}$ groups are at the $\\beta$-position with respect to the central ketone group). $\\beta$-keto acids readily undergo decarboxylation upon heating. Since there are two such groups, it will undergo double decarboxylation.<br><br>$\\text{HO}_2\\text{C}-\\text{CH}(\\text{CH}_3)-\\text{C}(=\\text{O})-\\text{CH}(\\text{CH}_3)-\\text{CO}_2\\text{H} \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{C}(=\\text{O})-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_3 + 2\\text{CO}_2$<br><br>The major product $\\textbf{X}$ is 3-pentanone. <br>Number of carbonyl groups ($>\\text{C=O}$) in $\\textbf{X} = 1$.<br><br>Reaction 2:<br>The reactant is *cis*-3,4-dicarboxycyclopentanone. The two carboxylic acid groups are on adjacent carbons (a 1,2-dicarboxylic acid system). Heating a 1,2-dicarboxylic acid leads to dehydration, forming a stable 5-membered cyclic anhydride.<br><br>The major product $\\textbf{Y}$ is the cyclic anhydride of the reactant, which is a fused bicyclic system containing the original cyclopentanone ring and a newly formed succinic anhydride ring.<br>The structure of $\\textbf{Y}$ contains one ketone carbonyl group and two anhydride carbonyl groups.<br>Number of carbonyl groups ($>\\text{C=O}$) in $\\textbf{Y} = 1 + 2 = 3$.<br><br>Sum of total number of carbonyl groups in $\\textbf{X}$ and $\\textbf{Y} = 1 + 3 = 4$.<br><br>Answer: $4$",
    "solution_image": null,
    "topic": "Reactions Involving Acid Group"
  },
